+\section{What This Project Is About}
+This repository is a beginner-to-intermediate quantitative finance and machine learning project focused on forecasting the price of U.S. wheat futures. The main idea is to see whether historical wheat prices, together with macroeconomic indicators, can be used to predict future price movements.
+
+In simple terms, the project asks this question:
+
+\begin{quote}
+Can we use past market data and economic data to build a model that learns patterns and helps forecast the next wheat futures price?
+\end{quote}
+
+This is not a normal web app or a clean software product. It is closer to a research project, where the goal is to experiment with data, test models, compare approaches, and evaluate whether the predictions are useful.
+
+\section{Motivation}
+The motivation of the project comes from three connected ideas:
+
+\subsection{1. Commodity Prices Matter}
+Wheat is an important agricultural commodity. Its price is affected by supply, demand, inflation, trade, energy prices, interest rates, and broader economic conditions. Because of that, wheat futures can be studied using both market data and macroeconomic data.
+
+\subsection{2. Financial Time Series Are Hard}
+Forecasting prices is difficult because prices change over time, contain noise, and react to many factors at once. A project like this tries to handle that difficulty by combining:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item historical price behavior,
+  \item economic indicators,
+  \item machine learning models built for sequential data.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{3. Learning Quant Research Workflow}
+This repo also looks like an attempt to learn or demonstrate a quant-style workflow:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item collect data,
+  \item clean and align it,
+  \item create training examples,
+  \item train forecasting models,
+  \item evaluate whether the models are better than simple baselines.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Main Goal}
+The practical goal is to forecast future wheat futures prices using:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item wheat futures historical price data,
+  \item macroeconomic indicators from FRED-MD and related sources,
+  \item deep learning models such as CNNs, RCNNs, and attention-based models.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{What Is In This Repo}
+The repo has two main forms of work:
+
+\subsection{Notebook-Based Experimentation}
+The notebook \texttt{AI\_ML\_Quants.ipynb} is the main exploratory workflow. It loads data, merges datasets, prepares time-series windows, and trains forecasting models.
+
+\subsection{Large Standalone Research Script}
+The file \texttt{research\_grade\_forecasting.py} is a much larger framework-style script. It includes reusable classes for:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item data processing,
+  \item model building,
+  \item training,
+  \item evaluation,
+  \item visualization,
+  \item backtesting.
+\end{itemize}
+
+This means the repo has both an experimental side and a more formal research-framework side.
+
+\section{Topics Covered in This Project}
+Below is a topic map of what the repo contains.
+
+\subsection{Data Topics}
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item CSV data loading with Python and pandas
+  \item date parsing and time indexing
+  \item merging multiple data sources
+  \item daily price data and macroeconomic features
+  \item handling missing values
+  \item feature alignment across different time frequencies
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{Finance and Economics Topics}
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item wheat futures
+  \item commodity price forecasting
+  \item macroeconomic indicators
+  \item interest rates
+  \item inflation-related indicators
+  \item trade and production indicators
+  \item financial market context
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{Machine Learning Topics}
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item supervised learning
+  \item regression
+  \item feature scaling
+  \item train/test splitting
+  \item time-series cross-validation
+  \item baseline comparison
+  \item hyperparameter tuning with Optuna
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{Deep Learning Topics}
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item TensorFlow/Keras models
+  \item PyTorch models
+  \item 1D convolutional neural networks (CNNs)
+  \item recurrent models such as GRU or RCNN-style designs
+  \item self-attention
+  \item temporal attention pooling
+  \item sequence modeling
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{Evaluation Topics}
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item RMSE and regression metrics
+  \item naive baseline forecasting
+  \item walk-forward or time-aware validation
+  \item comparing models
