+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item collect data,
+  \item clean the data,
+  \item align the data in time,
+  \item convert the data into model inputs,
+  \item train models,
+  \item evaluate the predictions.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Step 1: Load the Data}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The notebook reads price data for U.S. wheat futures and reads macroeconomic feature data from CSV files.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Machine learning models cannot work without structured input data. Price data gives the target behavior we want to predict, while macroeconomic data gives possible explanatory signals.
+
+\subsection{Why both kinds of data are used}
+Using only price data means the model only sees market history. Using macroeconomic features gives the model more context, such as interest rates, inflation, trade conditions, and broader economic signals.
+
+\section{Step 2: Clean and Standardize the Data}
+\subsection{What happens}
+Dates are parsed, rows are sorted by time, columns are converted to numeric types, and unusable values are removed or handled.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Real-world data is messy. If the dates are wrong, if numbers are stored as text, or if there are missing values everywhere, the model will learn from bad inputs. Cleaning makes the dataset usable and consistent.
+
+\section{Step 3: Align Different Time Frequencies}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The macroeconomic data and the wheat futures data are aligned into a shared timeline so that each prediction example uses the correct historical information.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+This is one of the most important steps. Financial and macro data often come at different frequencies. If they are not aligned properly, the model may accidentally use future information. That is called data leakage, and it makes results look better than they really are.
+
+\subsection{Why the project shifts features}
+Some macro features are shifted forward so that only information available at the time would be used for prediction. This is meant to make the experiment more realistic.
+
+\section{Step 4: Build the Combined Dataset}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The price series and the macro feature table are joined into one dataframe. After that, the project has one table where each time point contains both target price information and explanatory variables.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Models need one consistent input structure. Joining the datasets makes it possible to feed all relevant information into the forecasting pipeline.
+
+\section{Step 5: Create Lookback Windows}
+\subsection{What happens}
+Instead of giving the model one row at a time, the project builds sequences. For example, it may give the model the past 30 days of information and ask it to predict the next price.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Time-series problems depend on order and history. A single row usually does not carry enough context. A lookback window gives the model recent behavior, trends, and short-term temporal structure.
+
+\subsection{Why this matters so much}
+This is the step where an ordinary table becomes a sequence-learning problem. Once you create windows, models like CNNs, GRUs, and attention layers become useful.
+
+\section{Step 6: Split Training and Test Data by Time}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The data is split into earlier data for training and later data for testing.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done this way}
+In time series, you must not randomly shuffle data like in many ordinary machine learning problems. The future should not leak into the past. Training on older data and testing on newer data is a more realistic setup.
+
+\section{Step 7: Scale the Features}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The project applies scaling, such as standardization, to the model inputs and sometimes the targets.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Neural networks usually train more reliably when features are on compatible numeric scales. Without scaling, one variable might dominate just because of its raw magnitude.
+
+\subsection{Why scaling must be fit on training data only}
+If scaling is fitted on the full dataset, then the test set influences the preprocessing. That is another form of leakage. The correct way is:
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item fit the scaler on training data,
+  \item apply it to validation and test data.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Step 8: Train Forecasting Models}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The notebook builds deep learning models such as:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item CNN-based models,
+  \item RCNN-style models,
+  \item attention-enhanced models.
+\end{itemize}
+
