+1D CNNs can detect local temporal patterns in sequential data. In this problem, they can learn short-term movement structures from recent history.
+
+\subsection{Why recurrent or RCNN-style models are used}
+Recurrent layers are good for sequence dependence. They try to capture how information evolves across time rather than only seeing local patterns.
+
+\subsection{Why attention is used}
+Attention gives the model a way to focus more on some time steps than others. Instead of treating every past observation equally, the model can learn which moments are more important for prediction.
+
+\subsection{Why multiple models are tried}
+In research work, there is usually no guarantee that one architecture is best. Trying multiple models helps compare tradeoffs and see whether added complexity actually helps.
+
+\section{Step 9: Use Baselines and Validation}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The project compares models against simple baselines such as persistence forecasting and also uses time-series cross-validation in some parts.
+
+\subsection{Why this is necessary}
+If a complicated neural network cannot beat a very simple baseline, then the complex model may not be worth using. Baselines keep the project honest.
+
+\subsection{Why time-series cross-validation is used}
+It tests the model across multiple chronological splits instead of trusting one lucky train/test split. This gives a more stable view of performance.
+
+\section{Step 10: Tune Hyperparameters}
+\subsection{What happens}
+Some notebook sections use Optuna to search over hyperparameters such as dropout, number of filters, hidden dimensions, and learning rate.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Deep learning performance depends strongly on these settings. Hyperparameter tuning is a structured way to search for better choices instead of guessing.
+
+\subsection{Why beginners should be careful here}
+Hyperparameter tuning is useful, but it can distract beginners from the core ideas. If you do not yet understand the pipeline, tuning will feel like pressing random buttons.
+
+\section{Step 11: Evaluate the Results}
+\subsection{What happens}
+The project computes forecast metrics such as RMSE and compares predicted values with actual values.
+
+\subsection{Why it is done}
+Training loss alone is not enough. We need a clear measurement of how good the predictions are on unseen future data.
+
+\section{The Role of the Large Python Script}
+\subsection{What it is}
+The file \texttt{research\_grade\_forecasting.py} is a larger, more structured version of the same general idea. It contains:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item configuration classes,
+  \item data processors,
+  \item several model architectures,
+  \item trainer and evaluator classes,
+  \item visualization and backtesting utilities.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{Why it exists}
+Notebook code is useful for experimentation, but large projects become hard to maintain inside notebooks. A script with classes and reusable components is easier to extend and organize for more serious research.
+
+\subsection{Why it feels harder}
+It is written more like a framework than a tutorial. That is useful for advanced work, but not ideal for a beginner who is still learning the concepts.
+
+\section{How a Beginner Should Use This Repo}
+The best beginner workflow is:
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item ignore the big PyTorch script at first,
+  \item focus on the notebook data loading and preprocessing,
+  \item understand the combined dataset,
+  \item build one very simple baseline,
+  \item learn one deep model only,
+  \item return to attention and tuning later.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\section{Why the Repo Is Built This Way}
+This repo is built this way because the author seems to be solving a research problem, not writing a teaching tool. In research-style code, people often:
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item experiment quickly in notebooks,
+  \item add more advanced models over time,
+  \item compare many approaches,
+  \item refactor some ideas into reusable scripts later.
+\end{itemize}
+
+That is why the repo feels mixed: part exploratory notebook, part larger ML framework.
+
+\section{Final Beginner Summary}
+If you want the clearest possible mental model, use this:
+
+\begin{quote}
+The project takes past wheat prices and macroeconomic indicators, turns them into time windows, trains neural networks to predict future prices, and then checks whether those predictions are actually better than simple alternatives.
+\end{quote}
+
+That is the whole workflow. Everything else in the repo is a variation or extension of that basic pipeline.
+
+\end{document}
